this video is inevitable, If I made a top 10&nbsp;
best episodes video then I have to do the flip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:28.320
side. So get ready to feast on my misery as we&nbsp;
dive into the worst episodes of Game of Thrones.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:35.940
Number 10 - Season 6, Episode 1: The Red Woman.&nbsp;
This might be a bit surprising to some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.680
that season 6 is leaking onto this list, but&nbsp;
after revisiting this episode during my season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.160
6 devoid of logic videos, I relived how bad this&nbsp;
episode was. Almost every scene here has a glaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.900
writing issue that doesn’t make any sense. From&nbsp;
the whole situation with Ser Alliser allowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.400
someone to take Jon’s body and then getting mad&nbsp;
about it, along with him openly admitting that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.800
killed Jon. Sansa and Theon conveniently getting&nbsp;
saved by Brienne when she was busy with Stannis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.140
The power grab in Dorne is extremely inconsistent&nbsp;
since Ellaria Sand was literally just detained by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.000
guards for a potential coup and now they’re A&nbsp;
OK with killing Doran. The Sand Snakes being on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.860
the ship with Jaime, Myrcella, and Trystane and&nbsp;
for some reason they let Jaime go despite them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.660
killing Trystane. The Sons of the Harpy being&nbsp;
able to burn Dany’s entire fleet without anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.580
noticing. The Dothraki going out of their way to&nbsp;
capture Dany when they didn’t know who she was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:32.940
Jesus Christ the writing tanks so hard in this&nbsp;
episode and I’m surprised that we were so blind to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.800
this when it first came out. Like there’s really&nbsp;
nothing to be enjoyed here and even the dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.900
is at an all-time low. I was considering putting&nbsp;
an episode from the beginning of season 7 here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.160
but at least there’s a scene or two that are&nbsp;
at least entertaining within those episodes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.680
Then The Red Woman ends in the most scaringly&nbsp;
disgusting way possible in which Melissandre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.060
takes off her necklace and we see her&nbsp;
shriveled-up ballsack of a corpse. Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.580
we really need to see this? Not to mention that&nbsp;
this is also inconsistent with past scenes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.940
which she didn’t have her necklace on and she&nbsp;
didn’t turn into the old lady from Spongebob.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:11.400
This is a prime early example of David and Dan&nbsp;
struggling to write an episode from scratch.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:19.980
Number 9 - Season 5, Episode 6: Unbowed, Unbent,&nbsp;
Unbroken. Despite the terrible scenes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.020
transpire within this episode that we’ll talk&nbsp;
about in a second, 25% of this episode is made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.300
up of thoroughly engaging scenes of Arya doing&nbsp;
chores. This is a point where I’m sure this whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.220
development is way better in the books because you&nbsp;
can have Arya’s internal monologue and extensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.080
thought process while in the House of Black and&nbsp;
White. But in the show, you obviously can’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.980
that and she doesn’t have another character for&nbsp;
her to talk to and express what she’s thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:49.500
That’s why to me, this whole development with her&nbsp;
here is so boring. Along with Jaquen seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.720
jumping the gun and immediately showing Arya the&nbsp;
great hall with all the faces. Even though she is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.340
still failing her tests. So that’s a big chunk&nbsp;
of the episode that most people will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.860
skip altogether. The crazy thing is that the&nbsp;
episode gets progressively worse as it goes on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.540
Like we then go to Tyrion and Ser Jorah getting&nbsp;
captured by pirates and they consider chopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.760
off Tyrion’s cock. Which is just such a jarringly&nbsp;
weird dialogue scene and when you compare this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.440
Tyrion outsmarting the mountain tribe it’s such&nbsp;
a massive difference in quality. In one scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.480
you have Tyrion taking advantage of the low IQ of&nbsp;
his captors and promising them riches, along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.640
defusing all the tension with his well-executed&nbsp;
humor. In the other, Tyrion just says that he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:36.900
a big cock. So yeahhhh. And also do cock merchants&nbsp;
even exist? Then Littlefinger scheming is baffling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.640
and has no semblance of coherency since he gave&nbsp;
Sansa to the Boltons and now he wants to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.800
warden of the North by attacking the boltons.&nbsp;
And apart of this plan between him and Cersei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.200
is he’ll kill Sansa, so right away this scene is&nbsp;
pointless because we know that won’t happen. David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.640
and Dan were just like oh, we have a bare minimum&nbsp;
requirement to make it seem as if Littlefinger is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.920
still smart and is scheming. The episode keeps&nbsp;
spiraling with the terrible climax of the Dorne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.440
plotline and any action here is poorly executed&nbsp;
with tons of cutting. It also doesn’t make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.240
how these soldiers choose not to turn when&nbsp;
they do so a few episodes later in season 6;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.000
how convenient for Jaime who’s infiltrating Dorne.&nbsp;
The final scene of the episode is probably one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.560
the most abhorrent moments in the show in&nbsp;
which Ramsay rapes Sansa. We really didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.600
need to see this and felt more like David and Dan&nbsp;
injecting shock value to try and increase ratings.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:36.420
Number 8 - Season 7, Episode 5: Eastwatch. Oh boy,&nbsp;
I love opening an episode by getting smacked in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:41.640
the face with a big schlong of plot armor. Bronn&nbsp;
must be an Olympic free diver if he was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.860
swim Jaime while he was in full armor across&nbsp;
an entire lake. It’s very clear that David and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.420
Dan wrote themselves into a corner by including&nbsp;
Jaime and Bronn in this battle and needed some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:57.120
way for them to escape. And they didn’t even try&nbsp;
to make it make sense. Then Dickon randomly just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.320
kills himself and I feel like David and Dan were&nbsp;
probably thinking “I don’t know what to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.880
this character, let’s just kill him screw it”. And&nbsp;
then they try to play this off as Daenerys being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:11.280
unreasonable, like ok. One of the main reasons&nbsp;
why I’m including this episode within my list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.220
is because of how absurdly fast each character&nbsp;
was able to travel around Westeros. Just in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:22.560
episode alone, we go from this general area to&nbsp;
Dragonstone, from Dragonstone to Kingslanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.620
from Kingslanding back to Dragonstone, and then&nbsp;
from Dragonstone all the way to the goddamn wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:34.380
This destroys any semblance of space within this&nbsp;
world because it destroys all logic in how long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.880
it would take to travel to these locations.&nbsp;
The main way to make it seem natural would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.800
be to have in-between scenes or even episodes&nbsp;
to pad out that time, but David and Dan gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:49.020
up and cranked up the pace. It’s also funny to&nbsp;
me how Dany returns to Dragonstone on Drogon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.580
but somehow the Dothraki made it back before&nbsp;
her because these people were present during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.500
Loot Train attack. The other big reason why this&nbsp;
episode is here is because of Tyrion’s brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.820
plan to kidnap a wight to convince Cersei to&nbsp;
help them. I don’t think I need to dive into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:07.620
why this mashed potatoes logic is coming out&nbsp;
of young Sheldon, because obviously it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.300
get everyone killed and Cersei would gladly let&nbsp;
the dead kill the north because that would be her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.280
best chances of winning at this point. From this&nbsp;
point on, it’s basically as if the dragon crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.680
is assembling the avengers as they zip around the&nbsp;
globe and it is even served up with marvel quips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:30.180
like Davos quoting the meme of Gendry endlessly&nbsp;
rowing. Before this, season 7 was mediocre and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.980
kind of bad but this episode self-mutilated&nbsp;
the potential of any redemption for the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:39.660
of the season from how horrendous the direction&nbsp;
David and Dan decided to take the story here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.520
While also fully and utterly compromising&nbsp;
Tyrion who was once my favorite character.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:52.440
Number 7 - Season 8, Episode 1: Winterfell.&nbsp;
This episode doesn’t have a lot of atrociously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.300
insulting scenes that make you want to light your&nbsp;
eyes on fire and then extinguish them in acid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.920
but the general writing is still coated in trash&nbsp;
and is apparent by this point that David and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.740
Dan stopped trying. Hell, the first line of the&nbsp;
season is Tyrion making a cock joke. Scenes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:12.480
you’d think should have nothing wrong with them,&nbsp;
still are being fumbled. Arya and Jon’s reunion is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.520
laughably short and lazily written, Kingslanding&nbsp;
is completely barren and since it’s just Cersei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.020
and Euron, David and Dan have no idea what they&nbsp;
should talk about other than sex and elephants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.880
oddly, Bronn is given an objective to kill Tyrion&nbsp;
and Jaime despite Cersei not actually wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.320
that so it puts his character in limbo the whole&nbsp;
season as he goes to the Bahamas for a vacation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.540
Theon just waltz into Euron’s fleet with zero&nbsp;
conflict and saves Yara, rendering that whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.820
plot point as anticlimactic, Jon and Daenyers&nbsp;
riding dragons is shockingly reduced to sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:46.380
satire when it should have been a triumphant&nbsp;
reveal, and Tormund and Edd conveniently run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.640
into each other despite it being illogical.&nbsp;
It feels like anyone could have reviewed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.380
script and addressed these issues, but the only&nbsp;
thing I can think of is David and Dan not caring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:00.720
Even some of the biggest moments feel dampened&nbsp;
by the poor dialogue. During the grand reveal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.940
Jon Snow being Aegon Targaryen he just resorts to&nbsp;
the copy and pasted dialogue that he repeats for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.800
the entire season. Not to mention that the scene&nbsp;
ends in less than a minute after Sam tells Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.840
the big news. If David and Dan weren’t rushing&nbsp;
through these scenes as fast as possible then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:21.060
they could have easily written 10 episodes worth.&nbsp;
This is kind of a larger issue within seasons 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:25.860
and 8 but it’s more noticeable for the episodes&nbsp;
that are primarily composed of dialogue scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.840
How am I supposed to form any attachment to these&nbsp;
scenes when you are quickly ending them with very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.640
little resolution and proper development. It feels&nbsp;
like we are just going down a checklist of plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.920
points to set up before getting to the big action&nbsp;
set-pieces. Instead of having any semblance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:45.660
being like the earlier seasons where each dialogue&nbsp;
scene was fully explored and engaged the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:50.520
with its riveting and complex characters.&nbsp;
Just thinking about the notion of Sansa and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.080
Jon having a scene together arguing makes me&nbsp;
sick to my stomach at this point in the story.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:09:03.720
Number 6 - Season 7, Episode 7: The Dragon and&nbsp;
the Wolf. The last 20 minutes of this episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:10.380
ranges from alright to good, but the first hour&nbsp;
is an absolutely miserable experience. We have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:14.580
forty-minute sequence of our now brain-dead&nbsp;
characters trying to have an intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.980
discussion. Starting off this forty-minute-long&nbsp;
nightmare, we have Bronn talking about unsullied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:25.620
who don’t have cocks. Ah yes, I sure do love the&nbsp;
topics that David and Dan decide to explore in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:31.440
their dialogue scenes. Then for the actual general&nbsp;
reunion, it never has any substance. From the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.140
blatant cleganebowl unskippable ad, Euron randomly&nbsp;
chiming in to make jokes, and Jon giving his Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.940
King speech for the one-hundredth time, this is&nbsp;
just terrible. For the amount of characters here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:46.560
it’s really sad that David and Dan weren’t able&nbsp;
to come up with a single memorable or even decent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.420
interaction. This also isn’t even mentioning&nbsp;
how this entire sequence has no point in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.640
overall story. Why is Cersei even entertaining&nbsp;
the notion that she cares about helping? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.140
are Jon and Dany going to do about her not&nbsp;
helping, kill her harder? Like what’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:05.880
point of Cersei bamboozling everyone here that&nbsp;
she’s going to help? It literally serves no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:10.680
purpose and is just a waste of time. If you were&nbsp;
to remove this scene and went right into season 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:15.960
almost nothing changes. The only key difference&nbsp;
is Jaime’s involvement, but you can still easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:20.700
write in a way for him to get upset and to help&nbsp;
the living. If anything, I’d prefer an arc where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:26.520
Jaime realizes how evil Cersei is and that aspect&nbsp;
slowly drives him away and he switches sides. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:31.500
because she lied. Given that Jaime’s overall arc&nbsp;
is basically the opposite of Cersei’s in that he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.120
becoming more honorable and morally good, while&nbsp;
Cersei is becoming more wicked and is pushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.500
further into that abyss through each death of her&nbsp;
kids. You’d think that the death of Tommen would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:44.280
have a bigger impact within their relationship&nbsp;
because Cersei was partially responsible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:49.500
but that whole aspect goes unexplored. Or even&nbsp;
just acknowledging the fact that she blew up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.900
sept. The Kingslanding narrative in this season&nbsp;
was so barebones already that this more developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.900 --> 00:11:00.240
dynamic between Cersei and Jaime was much needed.&nbsp;
Although Jaime’s arc works here in this episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.320
I think it could have been done better. So&nbsp;
taking out the factor of Jaime, It feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.060
like this entire sequence is to only justify&nbsp;
the reunion of Cersei and Tyrion so that David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:15.060
and Dan can try and utterly fail at re-living the&nbsp;
best of Game of Thrones’ past. And the sad thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:21.000
is that it worked. The critics ate that shit up&nbsp;
and the season received a bunch of awards. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.380
there’s one other crippling issue in this episode&nbsp;
and that’s the death of Littlefinger. Saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:30.480
David and Dan committed a character assassination&nbsp;
would be kind because this man has been done so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.040
dirty since the beginning of season 5. The more&nbsp;
you think about anything Littlefinger does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:39.840
the less it makes sense. And then to make&nbsp;
things even worse Bran just farts around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.460
and doesn’t bother with telling anyone that&nbsp;
Littlefinger is a menace to society. David and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.960
Dan yet again create a false sense of tension&nbsp;
because it’s completely illogical for Bran to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:54.780
withhold this critical information. This finale is&nbsp;
a massive waste of time and it also comes with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:59.220
side of David and Dan plunging a dagger into our&nbsp;
hearts for how they handled a beloved character.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:07.020
Number 5 - Season 7, Episode 6: Beyond the Wall.&nbsp;
This was the original point in the show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:11.400
shattered my illusion that Game of Thrones&nbsp;
was still a good show. Because before this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:15.660
the flaws were there, but they were somewhat&nbsp;
ignorable because they didn’t go absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:20.760
ape-shit in terms of terrible writing. The&nbsp;
inherent goal that David and Dan created where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:25.140
Jon and company have to go beyond the wall to&nbsp;
steal a wight is singlehandedly one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:29.700
stupid directions to take the story. I still don’t&nbsp;
know why they couldn’t have had Dany at least fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:34.740
them in and out for this mission instead of what&nbsp;
transpired. Or even Dany flying in to scoop up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:39.840
some wights in Drogon’s claws and bring them back&nbsp;
south. The fact that you can immediately with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:44.220
very little thought come up with a better plan&nbsp;
than the smartest character in the show is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:49.920
telling for how horrendous the whole episode is.&nbsp;
Then the execution of the episode writing-wise is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.960
even worse. The wights that end up chasing Jon’s&nbsp;
entourage end up getting stopped because the ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.940
broke, even though they have been shown to be&nbsp;
able to swim. Gendry, a man who grew up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:05.460
warm climate of Kingslanding, is able to somehow&nbsp;
run miles nonstop back to the wall without his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:10.560
lungs exploding in this extremely cold weather.&nbsp;
You’d think Tormund would be more suited for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:15.840
objective given that he grew up in this terrain.&nbsp;
Jon’s entourage constantly surviving certain death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:20.640
scenarios, Jon for whatever reason not wanting&nbsp;
to get on Drogon because he was getting carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:25.560
away killing wights, and then Jon miraculously&nbsp;
surviving and having Uncle Benjen come in like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:30.540
the eagles from Lord of the Rings to save him.&nbsp;
Did we need to have this moment for Uncle Benjen?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:35.880
Then this is padded out with the dumb conflict&nbsp;
of if Arya is going to kill Sansa. And Sansa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:40.680
is again listening to Littlefinger, someone who&nbsp;
she really should not be trusting by this point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:46.440
Almost every aspect of this episode sucks ass and&nbsp;
there may be a few moments in the beginning with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:51.600
some interesting dialogue, but it’s jarring to&nbsp;
constantly use cock jokes in almost every episode.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:59.340
Number 4 - Season 8, Episode 4: The&nbsp;
Last of the Starks. Oh boy here we go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:03.540
Between the last four episodes of season 8,&nbsp;
I hate this one the least because it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:08.160
absolutely butcher a major aspect that the show&nbsp;
has been building towards. But don’t get me wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:12.720
The Last of the Starks is still horrible. This&nbsp;
episode includes the single most jarring point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:17.700
in the entire show which is the death of Rhaegal.&nbsp;
The fact that David and Dan play this scene to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:23.100
surprise the audience as random shock value is&nbsp;
the most degenerate form of writing. A good twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:27.900
should be logical within the story at the very&nbsp;
least and should have a small thread of buildup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:32.940
Like the Red Wedding and the death of Ned Stark.&nbsp;
Here, it feels like David and Dan are in panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:38.280
mode as they have no idea how to match those highs&nbsp;
from the earlier seasons. What’s that? We were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:42.960
literally just reminded about Euron’s fleet the&nbsp;
scene prior? Oh, shouldn’t we send scouting ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:47.820
ahead so that we stop getting ambushed by Euron’s&nbsp;
fleet which ALREADY HAPPENED TWICE IN THE STORY!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:52.500
You’d think that they would learn from their&nbsp;
mistakes, but David and Dan throw out all logic in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:57.540
the story to bend and break it at their own will.&nbsp;
This time to the complete detriment of one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:02.580
Dany’s dragons that the audience has come to love&nbsp;
through the years. And this is just one scene in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:07.500
the episode. The vast majority of the celebration&nbsp;
plays like a sitcom because David and Dan find it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:12.960
easier to write quote-on-quote comedy rather than&nbsp;
Drama. Along with it including this classic line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:18.180
“I don’t really want any more”. Which perfectly&nbsp;
sets up the ending in which he wants to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:23.100
the king. “Why do you think I came all this&nbsp;
way.” The romance between Jaime and Brienne felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:28.020
really awkward to me, Jon is stuck in his never&nbsp;
ending loop of saying muh queen and I dun wan et,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:33.300
Bronn randomly comes in out of nowhere to then&nbsp;
exit the story again, and Missendei somehow gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:38.820
captured by Euron with a dash of coughing varys.&nbsp;
I am leaving out one scene that upsets me more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:44.040
than these which is Jon telling Arya and Sansa&nbsp;
his true identity. This again highlights David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:49.200
and Dan’s utter laziness in writing because they&nbsp;
don’t even bother to show us critical reactions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:53.880
his closest family members. Like this should be&nbsp;
the best scene of the episode and David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:59.640
somehow fumble the easiest touchdown in existence.&nbsp;
Then the final scene is so bizarre where Dany’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:03.780
support group shows up to Cersei’s doorstep&nbsp;
to beg them to surrender. This essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:09.000
resembles the finale of season 7 where everyone&nbsp;
knows the outcome of this scene and it ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:14.100
being pointless. But the reason why this moment&nbsp;
is worse is because Cersei has the means to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:19.740
the war right now. She has multiple scorpions&nbsp;
pointing at a motionless Drogon and could easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:24.540
barrage Dany’s whole group with arrows. Or they&nbsp;
could literally just open the gates to reveal the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:29.520
golden company that then slaughters them. Cersei&nbsp;
doesn’t have Jaime’s good moral conscious to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:34.260
her back and I’m surprised Euron didn’t encourage&nbsp;
her to slaughter them all here. But yet again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:39.180
this is another contrived scenario so Tyrion&nbsp;
could give an emmy baiting monologue to Cersei.

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:46.260
Number 3 - Season 8, Episode 3: The Long&nbsp;
Night. If I were to go back and remake my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:51.540
devoid of logic videos for season 8, I’d find the&nbsp;
most material within this episode from how crazily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:56.820
brainless it all is. So lets go through a quick&nbsp;
laundry list of everything I can think of that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:02.760
stupid in this episode. Jon and Dany waiting an&nbsp;
unnecessarily long time to do strafe runs on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:08.040
army of the dead, the Dothraki randomly charging&nbsp;
the enemy despite being told not to, the layout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:12.480
of the army by having the trebuchets in front&nbsp;
of your soldiers and not digging more trenches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:17.760
our frontline of heroes miraculously surviving&nbsp;
a literal wave of undead that washed over them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:22.680
our non-fighting heroes hiding in a crypt that&nbsp;
famously has corpses in them, not creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:27.933
failsafe mechanic to light the trench on fire if a&nbsp;
dragon becomes incappacited, not having any siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.933 --> 00:17:32.340
like materials like pouring oil on the dead, and&nbsp;
Jon being able to keep fighting after crashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:37.980
on Rhaegal and being A OK along with being&nbsp;
surrounded by wights and surviving. Also not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:42.960
to exaggerate but almost every instance in which&nbsp;
our characters are fighting the undead, they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.520
put into scenarios where they should have died.&nbsp;
I think everyone was surrounded at some point and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:52.560
the wights decide to hug them instead of instantly&nbsp;
stabbing them like they did to the extras. Let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:57.840
alone Sam the Slayer wrestling the undead in the&nbsp;
mud. You can’t go a few minutes in The Long Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:03.180
without being visually assaulted by the abysmal&nbsp;
writing and having plot armor permeate in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:07.917
single moment. What a great way to destroy any&nbsp;
form of tension when everyone is immortal now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.917 --> 00:18:09.720
This was once a show where a character died from&nbsp;
an infection. Then that isn’t even bringing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:14.940
light or should I say darkness the fact that you&nbsp;
can’t see anything. I get that this battle takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:20.400
place at night but any example of night shoots for&nbsp;
battles has tangible lighting in them and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:25.560
always use the moon as a general light source.&nbsp;
Also despite this entire episode being an action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:30.720
set piece, there was only a couple choreographed&nbsp;
extended fight scenes like Arya fighting up on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:35.940
wall. Besides that, all the action is just quick&nbsp;
shots of people randomly swinging at wights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:40.740
that quickly gets repetitive. So since we covered&nbsp;
all that shite, we can now finally talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:45.900
ending. The fact that David and Dan are basing the&nbsp;
entirety of this moment on subverting expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:51.300
instead of a proper moment of payoff that has&nbsp;
been built up to for years is mind-boggling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:56.040
It’s very clear that David and Dan didn’t know&nbsp;
what to do with Arya later in the season so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.040 --> 00:19:00.300
probably panicked and gave her the Night King.&nbsp;
Even though her killing the Night King doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:05.280
actually utilize any of the skills she acquired&nbsp;
because she literally just rushes in for the kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:11.100
or fly I should say. This episode manages to&nbsp;
completely squander the single biggest aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:16.080
the show that so many people were hyped to see and&nbsp;
was the major moment where the casuals realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:21.960
how bad the show was. Because up until this point,&nbsp;
the show was still getting good reviews on IMDB.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:29.700
Number 2 - Season 8, Episode 5: The Bells. This&nbsp;
episode is like having the foresight that a train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:34.920
is about to crash and you are powerless to stop&nbsp;
it and you have to witness it in slow motion. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:40.020
sure this episode wouldn’t be absolutely terrible&nbsp;
if David and Dan bothered to do some real writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:45.000
and develop these characters and have them make&nbsp;
realistic decisions based in logic. With real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:49.140
seasons composed of 10 episodes that don’t&nbsp;
just rush through the plot points in an any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:54.120
percent speedrun, then I’m sure this moment in&nbsp;
the story would make sense. But the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:58.980
Dany wins the war with essentially no losses&nbsp;
and then decides to snap for no good reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:03.840
is baffling. It creates an immediate disconnect&nbsp;
with her character because the writers are forcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:07.860
her to do something that isn’t properly set&nbsp;
up and are only filling in a plot point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:12.300
George RR. Martin told them that happens. Then&nbsp;
we are left to sit here and witness this shit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:16.740
for the remainder of the episode. It’s really&nbsp;
hard to enjoy this spectacle when it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:22.080
feel earned in any way. That’s why I can’t give&nbsp;
it any redeeming qualities. Not to mention that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:26.940
there are still dumb aspects like the scorpions&nbsp;
suddenly not working. Like in every wide shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:31.320
that shows the scorpions, they are always&nbsp;
motionless and seem like they aren’t manned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:35.880
Which is a massive difference from the episode&nbsp;
before, that had Rhaegal get head shotted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:41.280
miles away while moving. And of course, all this&nbsp;
is accompanied by other disgustingly incompetent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:45.840
writing choices like Jaime saying he doesn’t care&nbsp;
for the innocents which broke his character arc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:50.760
Euron coincidentally swimming to shore at&nbsp;
the exact spot and time Jaime is there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:56.100
Arya suddenly transforming into a cat because she&nbsp;
now has nine lives, and Jaime and Cersei dying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:00.780
a bunch of bricks. When literally any other&nbsp;
fan theory that predicts the fate of Cersei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:04.740
and Jaime is better than what you were able&nbsp;
to come up with, then you know that you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:09.600
horrible writers. Every now and then you’d find&nbsp;
maybe one of these egregious writing mistakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:14.520
in the last couple of seasons like Arya getting&nbsp;
stabbed multiple times and living in season 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:20.640
but here, writing mistakes of that caliber&nbsp;
happen are in every other scene. The only thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:25.080
here that is half entertaining is clegane bowl&nbsp;
but even that feels like fan service in a way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:29.820
I feel really bad for the actual production&nbsp;
side of things because it’s a complete waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:34.920
of talent in having all of these amazing effects&nbsp;
and huge sets bring to life an atrocious script.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:43.620
Number 1 - Season 8, Episode 6: The Iron Throne.&nbsp;
By this point in season 8 I was inconsolable. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:48.120
sat there like a lifeless husk as I witnessed&nbsp;
my favorite show succumb to some of the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:53.580
writing I’ve ever witnessed. So does it get&nbsp;
better from here? “Absolutely fucking not.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:59.040
Not only does this episode have a plethora of&nbsp;
god-awful moments, it’s also overly drawn out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:04.020
with filler. We have to watch every character&nbsp;
slowly walk through Kingslanding as if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:09.840
is some deeply moving material and Tyrion moves&nbsp;
chairs around like an ADHD maniac. David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:15.000
are in full crisis mode as HBO and the audience&nbsp;
expects them to output episodes over an hour long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:20.220
so they just give you drool-inducing scenes that&nbsp;
don’t engage the audience in any way. Like what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:25.080
was the point of this scene where Greyworm was&nbsp;
executing soldiers and clearly was so far gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:29.520
that he didn’t care anymore. Shouldn’t this be&nbsp;
setting up a showdown of sorts between Greyworm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:34.740
and Jon after Jon kills Dany? Since Greyworm&nbsp;
clearly wouldn’t let Jon live for doing that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:38.940
It seems like this scene was on the cutting room&nbsp;
floor because David and Dan didn’t even bother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:43.200
with the notion of Greyworm teleporting to the&nbsp;
entrance of the Red Keep when Jon was walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:47.820
there the whole time. Then I’m sure the death of&nbsp;
Daenerys would have been enjoyable at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:52.800
but the audience is so emotionally detached from&nbsp;
the insane roller coaster of dreadful writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:58.200
that this scene comes across as soulless. Oh hey,&nbsp;
Drogon are you here to give Jon some consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:03.780
for killing your mother? Oh, you’re not, well&nbsp;
ok then. This leads into what I’d consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:08.640
worst scene in the show which is the election of&nbsp;
the new king. Everyone knows what’s wrong here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:13.140
but it’s still hilarious to me that David and&nbsp;
Dan try to pass off stories making a good king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:19.500
as if it’s some kind of big and smart revelation.&nbsp;
When it just comes across as absurdly stupid. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:23.820
somehow David and Dan keep face-planting with&nbsp;
the writing in regards to the Iron Islands not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:29.340
wanting independence, Greyworm allowing Tyrion&nbsp;
to give a monologue, Bran actually wanting it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:35.400
the scene including the now sadly normal flare&nbsp;
of sitcom comedy, and an incompressibly worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:40.620
method of electing a king. Do you realize how&nbsp;
much more corrupt this process will be if it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:45.660
an election process between the elites? You&nbsp;
will have to deal with intimidation, bribery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:51.360
and flat-out assassination attempts in order to&nbsp;
gain votes to become king. This system is so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:55.740
worse to the point that it’s disrespectful to&nbsp;
Dany’s memory. Even though she wanted to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.740 --> 00:24:01.020
the wheel by essentially making an even bigger&nbsp;
wheel I guess, so I don’t know. The Iron Throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:05.220
is an excruciating ending to season 8 and now&nbsp;
they want to continue the story from here with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:10.620
Jon. I wish the best of luck to those writers&nbsp;
because you have a colossal obstacle ahead of you.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:15.060
Those are my top 10 worst episodes of&nbsp;
Game of Thrones. I hope you enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:18.900
going down this cursed and appalling&nbsp;
memory lane of sorts. But yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:22.440
thank you for watching and let me know&nbsp;
what your top 10 worst episodes are.

